Mica is experimental and not ready for production. The API and the content format can still change before 1.0. This is what Mica does today, at 0.14.

## The model and the website

- You write the content model in TypeScript: collections, singletons and fields for text, rich text, media, relations, places on a map and more. `label` names the website for its editors.
- `@micacms/astro` turns the model into Astro collections. The build needs no server, no token and no network. Rich text is Markdoc, and images in it come out as optimised `srcset` images.

## Editing

- The local editor runs with `npx mica start`. It has no sign-in and listens only on your machine. `npx mica start --preview` also starts the website's `astro dev`.
- The self-hosted editor is one Docker container with OpenID Connect sign-in. Editors who are not developers use it. With Google, a domain rule admits only accounts of your Workspace domain.
- The editor saves by itself. A save is a draft. Publish makes one Git commit of the changes that the editor chooses.
- The live preview shows the real page of the website beside the form, with the drafts in it. A click on a text, an image, a date or an option in the page opens its field. The self-hosted editor shows it with the optional preview container `ghcr.io/micacms/preview`. The top of the menu shows the website's name and whether the preview is live.
- Coding agents can edit the content over MCP, and they follow the same rules as the editor. An agent needs your consent, and the consent page waits one second before it accepts Allow.

## Content in Git

- The content is files in your repository: JSON, Markdoc and media. Mica has no database and no content API.
- Large media can live in Git LFS. The content can live in a repository of its own, as a Git submodule.
- Reference sets let an entry link to data that Mica does not edit, such as a list of thousands of places.
- `mica check` tests every entry against the model, and `mica import` moves Astro collections into Mica.

## Checked and clear

- A broken entry file does not hide its collection. Mica names the file it cannot read. A full disk or a server failure gives a clear message with a reference that is also in the log.
- The System page lists the last warnings and errors since Mica started, and the state and the last lines of the preview. "Copy report" adds them, with no email addresses and no secrets.
- The tests run on Windows, macOS and Node.js 26, on the packed packages in a new Astro project, on the local editor in a browser, and on the self-hosted live preview in a browser.

## The details of each release

The notes of every 0.x release are in the [GitHub releases](https://github.com/micacms/mica/releases).
